* =============================================================================
* cplex.opt  --  RODeO Storage Dispatch (MIP)
* Tuned for rolling-window unit-commitment style MIP with binary on/off,
* startup, and cooldown variables (cooldown_separation_eqn formulation).
*
* CPLEX parameter reference:
*   https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/icos/22.1.1?topic=cplex-list-parameters
* =============================================================================


* ─── Scaling ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
* 1 = auto-scale (reduces numerical issues from mixed MWh/binary coefficients)
scaind        1


* ─── Root LP: barrier + dual crossover ───────────────────────────────────────
* Barrier (method 4) handles the dense cumulative-sum LP relaxation well.
* barcrossalg 1 = dual crossover after barrier: produces a clean simplex basis
* needed for warm-starting branch nodes.  Without crossover CPLEX cannot branch.
lpmethod      4
baritlim      10000
barcrossalg   1


* ─── Node LPs: dual simplex ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
* Dual simplex (2) re-optimizes the perturbed LP fastest after each branch.
subalg        2


* ─── Parallelism ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
* -1 = use all logical cores.  Each rolling-window solve is independent so
* CPLEX parallel B&B fills all cores within a single solve.
threads       -1


* ─── MIP gap tolerance ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
* Must match GAMS optcr = 0.05.  CPLEX stops when
*   (best_bound - best_incumbent) / (1e-10 + |best_incumbent|) < epgap.
epgap         0.05


* ─── Numerical tolerances ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
* epint 1e-5: slightly looser than default (1e-6) avoids false infeasibilities
*   caused by tiny floating-point residuals in binary variables.
* eprhs 1e-6: primal feasibility tolerance (default, kept explicit).
epint         1e-5
eprhs         1e-6


* ─── Presolve ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
* Aggressive presolve exploits the tight incremental formulation
* (cum_energy, cum_cooldown, rolling_cd) to fix/eliminate many variables
* before the first LP solve.
* Note: prelinind does not exist in CPLEX 22.x and was removed.
preind        1
aggind        4


* ─── Cut generation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
* These cut families are most effective for binary unit-commitment MIPs.
*
* cliques  2: clique cuts from binary conflict graph (output_active / input_active
*             cannot both be 1, cooldown_separation_eqn structure)
* covers   2: cover cuts from 0/1 knapsack rows (cycle-limit constraints)
* disjcuts 2: disjunctive cuts (very powerful for binary feasibility region)
* flowcovers 2: flow-cover cuts (energy balance constraints have network structure)
* mircuts  2: mixed-integer rounding (effective on cumulative energy rows)
* implbd   2: implied-bound cuts derived from logical implications between binaries
* gomcuts  2: Gomory fractional cuts at root (correct opt-file name)
* cutpass -1: let CPLEX auto-determine the number of cut rounds at root
cliques       2
covers        2
disjcuts      2
flowcovers    2
mircuts       2
implbd        2
gomcuts       2
cutpass       -1


* ─── Probing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
* Level 1: moderate probing.  Fixes binary variables by propagating implications
* (e.g., cooldown=1 forces output_active=0 for Tcycle intervals).
* Level 3 would be more powerful but too slow for large rolling windows.
probe         1


* ─── Node selection ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
* 1 = best-bound first: minimises the final optimality gap fastest.
* Use 2 (best-estimate) if you observe excessive memory use from a large tree.
nodesel       1


* ─── Variable branching direction ────────────────────────────────────────────
* 4 = pseudo-cost branching: balances branching quality vs overhead.
* Switch to 3 (strong branching) if operating_period_length <= 48 h and
* solve time per window is < 30 s.
varsel        4


* ─── Primal heuristics ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
* fpheur 2: aggressive feasibility pump.  Log showed first real incumbent only
*   found via polishing after 300 s; fpheur 2 finds a good start much sooner.
* rinsheur 5: run RINS every 5 B&B nodes.  Very effective for temporal MIPs
*   because the relaxation solution is close to a feasible dispatch schedule.
* mipemphasis 1: shift search toward finding feasibles faster.  Log showed
*   B&B exploring 400-1700 nodes with no useful incumbent before polishing.
fpheur        2
rinsheur      5
mipemphasis   1


* ─── Solution polishing ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
* After 120 s of B&B, if a feasible incumbent exists CPLEX switches to
* intensive local-search polishing to close the remaining gap quickly.
* Reduced from 300 s: log showed B&B not finding useful incumbents before
* polishing anyway, so triggering polishing sooner saves time.
polishaftertime  120


* ─── MIP warm start ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
* GAMS automatically writes variable .l values (from the previous rolling
* window) as an MST file.  mipstart 2 tells CPLEX to repair and use the
* start even if it is infeasible in the new window -- all 4 windows in the
* log showed "No solution found from 1 MIP starts" with mipstart 1.
mipstart      2


* ─── Symmetry breaking ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
* -1 = auto (CPLEX decides based on problem structure).
* If multiple identical devices are used, try symmetry 5 (aggressive).
symmetry      -1


* ─── Memory management ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
* workmem: RAM (MB) before CPLEX writes B&B tree nodes to disk.
*   16384 = 16 GB.  Machine has 32 GB physical RAM.
* nodefileind 0: keep all nodes in RAM (no disk writes).  Machine has 32 GB;
*   log showed trees of 40-64 MB which fit easily in RAM.
workmem       16384
nodefileind   0


* ─── Diagnostic output ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
* mipinterval 100: print MIP progress every 100 nodes (0 = every node, too verbose)
* mipdisplay  4: show cut summary and heuristic progress
mipinterval   100
mipdisplay    4


* =============================================================================
* TUNING GUIDE
* -----------
* Current settings are tuned for 6-hr window (Sens_dur_6hrs) on a 32 GB /
* 12-core machine.  Tuned 2026-06-02 based on log analysis.
*
* If gap closes well but you want tighter results (< 3% gap):
*   mipemphasis 2   (switch back to optimality focus once feasibles are fast)
*   polishaftertime 180
*
* If solve is STILL slow to find first feasible:
*   fpheur 2        (already set; try increasing rinsheur to 3)
*
* If running on a small machine (< 8 cores, < 16 GB RAM):
*   threads 4
*   workmem 1024
*   nodefileind 2
*
* If multiple identical storage devices (devices_instance > 1):
*   symmetry 5
* =============================================================================
